{"repositoryId":1254593603,"availability":"available","full":"QumirLabs/qumirdb","name":"qumirdb","description":"QumirDB is an analytical query engine designed to run parquet-backed workloads","category":"Design","language":"C++","stars":9,"forks":1,"created":"2026-05-30T19:07:12Z","updated":"2026-10-17T16:02:32Z","license":"BSD-2-Clause","branch":"master","homepage":"https://db.qumir.dev","topics":["database","sql"],"demo":"https://db.qumir.dev/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T18:53:07.470Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-17T18:53:07.470Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-17T18:53:07.470Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-17T18:53:07.470Z","stars":9,"forks":1}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["The same compiled query runs natively and in your browser.","QumirDB is an analytical query engine for Parquet. It handles scans, filters, joins, aggregates, and sort. A planner uses predicate pushdown, join reorder, and row-group pruning to plan each query.","Every query compiles through LLVM into a generated kernel. It does not run through an interpreter. The same codegen path builds host machine code and also builds WebAssembly. The same fused kernel runs on the server, or in the browser against OPFS-backed datasets. There is no separate browser engine to keep in sync with the native one."],"features":["One optimizer, two languages. SQL owns scans, predicate pushdown, join","Same query, two targets. The native path uses a JIT to build host","Not tied to Rust. Any language with an LLVM backend and a C ABI can use"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/QumirLabs/qumirdb/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"4c650e3a9c11459c531384479160ca84de96fcb0992e4ed177aee0b398e8176d","extractedAt":"2026-10-17T16:01:56.716Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T18:53:07.590Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"The same compiled query runs natively and in your browser.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/QumirLabs/qumirdb/blob/mast